xs, i, j), i - 1)\0A    }\0A\0Alet plum__swap[T] (xs: Array[T]) (i: Int) (j: Int): Array[T] =\0A    if i == j { xs } else {\0A        let a = xs[i];\0A        let b = xs[j];\0A        xs.set(i, b).set(j, a)\0A    }\0A\0A/// One element, or `None` from an empty array.\0Apub let Rng.choice[T] (r: Rng) (xs: Array[T]): (Rng, Option[T]) =\0A    if Array.is_empty(xs) { (r, None) } else {\0A        let (next, i) = Rng.int_range(r, 0, xs.len());\0A        (next, Some(xs[i]))\0A    }\0A\0A// --- String ordering ---\0A\0A/// Byte-lexicographic, which for UTF-8 IS codepoint order, so this\0A/// agrees with the real prelude's rune-by-rune comparison without\0A/// needing a `.runes()` decoder.\0Alet string_concat_all_raw (parts: Array[String]): String = \22\22\0A\0A/// Joins in ONE allocation. Building a large string by repeated\0A/// `.concat` is quadratic; this is what the compiler's own emitter\0A/// uses for anything that accumulates.\0Apub let Array.concat_all (parts: Array[String]): String = string_concat_all_raw(parts)\0A\0A/// Decimal text to the NEAREST double, via libc `strtod`.\0A///\0A/// `parse_number` below validates the grammar and finds where the\0A/// number ends, and that part was always right. What it got wrong was\0A/// the VALUE: building it as `mantissa * 10^exp` in floating point\0A/// accumulates rounding error, so `9.21258e-07` parsed to a double\0A/// one ulp away and `parse_float(x.to_string()) != x`. `to_string`\0A/// goes to real lengths to guarantee that round trip, retrying\0A/// at 15, 16 and 17 digits until `strtod` agrees, so the inverse\0A/// has to be the same `strtod`, not an approximation of it.\0A///\0A/// Found by a property test on 2026-08-25. The Rust interpreter\0A/// computed the identical wrong double, so `interp-check` agreed\0A/// with it and saw nothing.\0Alet parse_float_raw (s: String): Float = 0.0\0A\0Alet string_cmp_raw (a: String) (b: String): Int = 0\0A\0A/// Sorted by BYTE order, not by any locale's alphabet — so uppercase\0A/// sorts before lowercase, and accented letters sort after `z`.\0Apub let Array.sort_string (arr: Array[String]): Array[String] = Array.sort_by(arr, |a: String, b: String| a <= b)\0A\0A// --- Assertions ---\0A//\0A// Mirrored from the real prelude, message for message: a test that\0A// fails should read the same whichever compiler ran it.\0A\0A/// Stops the program unless `cond` holds.\0A///\0A/// For a condition the code depends on, not for input validation:\0A/// there is no way to catch this.\0Apub let assert (cond: Bool): Unit =\0A    if cond { () } else { panic_raw(\22assertion failed\22) }\0A\0A/// Stops the program unless the two are equal, printing BOTH — which\0A/// is the difference from `assert(a == b)` and the reason to prefer\0A/// it in a test.\0Apub let assert_eq[T] (a: T) (b: T): Unit =\0A    if a == b { () } else {\0A        panic_raw(\22assertion failed: left != right\5Cn  left:  \22.concat(a.to_string()).concat(\22\5Cn  right: \22).concat(b.to_string()))\0A    }\0A\0A/// Stops the program if the two are equal, printing them.\0Apub let assert_ne[T] (a: T) (b: T): Unit =\0A    if a != b { () } else {\0A        panic_raw(\22assertion failed: left == right\5Cn  left:  \22.concat(a.to_string()).concat(\22\5Cn  right: \22).concat(b.to_string()))\0A    }\0A\0A// --- Collections: Map and Set ---\0A\0A/// Mirrored from `crates/plumc/src/lib.rs`'s STDLIB_COLLECTIONS_SRC,\0A/// character for character. `Map` is a real hash map, so the mirror\0A/// has to be exact in one way that is easy to miss: bucket index is\0A/// `String.hash(..) % bucket_count`, which means `Map.keys` and\0A/// `Map.values` enumerate in HASH order. A hash that disagreed with\0A/// the real compiler's would print the same map in a different order.\0A/// `String.hash` is FNV-1a in the prelude, including the sign-bit mask\0A/// that a first attempt got wrong.\0Apub struct MapEntry[K, V] { key: K, value: V }\0A/// A hash map from `K` to `V`.\0A///\0A/// PERSISTENT: `Map.insert` returns a new map and leaves the original\0A/// alone, so a map can be shared without copying defensively. Keys\0A/// need `Eq`.\0Apub struct Map[K, V] { buckets: Array[Array[MapEntry[K, V]]], size: Int }\0A\0Alet MAP_INITIAL_BUCKETS: Int = 8\0Alet MAP_LOAD_FACTOR_NUM: Int = 3\0Alet MAP_LOAD_FACTOR_DEN: Int = 4\0A\0Alet map_make_buckets[K, V] (n: Int): Array[Array[MapEntry[K, V]]] = {\0A    let mut buckets = [];\0A    for i in 0..n { buckets = buckets.push([]); };\0A    buckets\0A}\0A\0Alet value_hash[T] (x: T): Int = String.hash(x.to_string())\0A\0Alet map_bucket_index[K] (k: K) (bucket_count: Int): Int = value_hash(k) % bucket_count\0A\0Alet map_bucket_find_index[K: Eq, V] (bucket: Array[MapEntry[K, V]]) (k: K) (i: Int): Int =\0A    if i >= bucket.len() { -1 }\0A    else if bucket[i].key == k { i }\0A    else { map_bucket_find_index(bucket, k, i + 1) }\0A\0A/// An empty map.\0Apub let Map.new[K, V] (): Map[K, V] = Map { buckets: map_make_buckets(MAP_INITIAL_BUCKETS), size: 0 }\0A\0Alet map_rehash[K, V] (m: Map[K, V]) (new_bucket_count: Int): Map[K, V] = {\0A    let mut new_buckets = map_make_buckets(new_bucket_count);\0A    for bucket in m.buckets {\0A        for entry in bucket {\0A            let idx = map_bucket_index(entry.key, new_bucket_count);\0A            new_buckets = new_buckets.set(idx, new_buckets[idx].push(entry));\0A        }\0A    };\0A    Map { buckets: new_buckets, size: m.size }\0A}\0A\0A/// A NEW map with `k` set to `v`, replacing any previous value. The\0A/// original is unchanged.\0Apub let Map.insert[K: Eq, V] (m: Map[K, V]) (k: K) (v: V): Map[K, V] = {\0A    let idx = map_bucket_index(k, m.buckets.len());\0A    let bucket = m.buckets[idx];\0A    let existing_i = map_bucket_find_index(bucket, k, 0);\0A    let inserted = if existing_i >= 0 {\0A        Map { buckets: m.buckets.set(idx, bucket.set(existing_i, MapEntry { key: k, value: v })), size: m.size }\0A    } else {\0A        Map { buckets: m.buckets.set(idx, bucket.push(MapEntry { key: k, value: v })), size: m.size + 1 }\0A    };\0A    if inserted.size * MAP_LOAD_FACTOR_DEN > inserted.buckets.len() * MAP_LOAD_FACTOR_NUM {\0A        map_rehash(inserted, inserted.buckets.len() * 2)\0A    } else {\0A        inserted\0A    }\0A}\0A\0A/// The value for `k`, or `None`.\0Apub let Map.get[K: Eq, V] (m: Map[K, V]) (k: K): Option[V] = {\0A    let bucket = m.buckets[map_bucket_index(k, m.buckets.len())];\0A    let i = map_bucket_find_index(bucket, k, 0);\0A    if i >= 0 { Some(bucket[i].value) } else { None }\0A}\0A\0A/// Whether `k` has a value.\0Apub let Map.contains[K: Eq, V] (m: Map[K, V]) (k: K): Bool =\0A    map_bucket_find_index(m.buckets[map_bucket_index(k, m.buckets.len())], k, 0) >= 0\0A\0A/// A new map without `k`. Removing an absent key is not an error.\0Apub let Map.remove[K: Eq, V] (m: Map[K, V]) (k: K): Map[K, V] = {\0A    let idx = map_bucket_index(k, m.buckets.len());\0A    let bucket = m.buckets[idx];\0A    let i = map_bucket_find_index(bucket, k, 0);\0A    if i >= 0 { Map { buckets: m.buckets.set(idx, bucket.remove(i)), size: m.size - 1 } } else { m }\0A}\0A\0A/// How many entries.\0Apub let Map.len[K, V] (m: Map[K, V]): Int = m.size\0A\0A/// Every key, in no particular order — buckets, not insertion order.\0Apub let Map.keys[K, V] (m: Map[K, V]): Array[K] = {\0A    let mut acc = [];\0A    for bucket in m.buckets { for entry in bucket { acc = acc.push(entry.key); } };\0A    acc\0A}\0A\0A/// Every value, in the same order as `Map.keys`.\0Apub let Map.values[K, V] (m: Map[K, V]): Array[V] = {\0A    let mut acc = [];\0A    for bucket in m.buckets { for entry in bucket { acc = acc.push(entry.value); } };\0A    acc\0A}\0A\0Alet map_from_arrays_acc[K: Eq, V] (keys: Array[K]) (values: Array[V]) (i: Int) (acc: Map[K, V]): Map[K, V] =\0A    if i >= keys.len() { acc } else { map_from_arrays_acc(keys, values, i + 1, Map.insert(acc, keys[i], values[i])) }\0A/// A map from parallel arrays, stopping at the shorter. A repeated\0A/// key keeps the LAST value.\0Apub let Map.from_arrays[K: Eq, V] (keys: Array[K]) (values: Array[V]): Map[K, V] = map_from_arrays_acc(keys, values, 0, Map.new(()))\0A\0A/// A set of `T`, persistent like `Map` — `Set.insert` returns a new\0A/// set. Members need `Eq`.\0Apub struct Set[T] { inner: Map[T, Unit] }\0A\0A/// An empty set.\0Apub let Set.new[T] (): Set[T] = Set { inner: Map.new(()) }\0A/// A new set with `x` added. Adding a member twice changes nothing.\0Apub let Set.insert[T: Eq] (s: Set[T]) (x: T): Set[T] = Set { inner: Map.insert(s.inner, x, ()) }\0A/// Whether `x` is a member.\0Apub let Set.contains[T: Eq] (s: Set[T]) (x: T): Bool = Map.contains(s.inner, x)\0A/// A new set without `x`.\0Apub let Set.remove[T: Eq] (s: Set[T]) (x: T): Set[T] = Set { inner: Map.remove(s.inner, x) }\0A/// How many members.\0Apub let Set.len[T] (s: Set[T]): Int = Map.len(s.inner)\0A/// Every member, in no particular order.\0Apub let Set.to_array[T] (s: Set[T]): Array[T] = Map.keys(s.inner)\0A\0A/// Everything in either set.\0Apub let Set.union[T: Eq] (a: Set[T]) (b: Set[T]): Set[T] = {\0A    let mut result = b;\0A    for x in Set.to_array(a) { result = Set.insert(result, x); };\0A    result\0A}\0A\0A/// Only what is in both.\0Apub let Set.intersection[T: Eq] (a: Set[T]) (b: Set[T]): Set[T] = {\0A    let mut result = Set.new(());\0A    for x in Set.to_array(a) { if Set.contains(b, x) { result = Set.insert(result, x); } };\0A    result\0A}\0A\0A/// What is in `a` and not in `b`.\0Apub let Set.difference[T: Eq] (a: Set[T]) (b: Set[T]): Set[T] = {\0A    let mut result = Set.new(());\0A    for x in Set.to_array(a) { if !Set.contains(b, x) { result = Set.insert(result, x); } };\0A    result\0A}\0A\0Alet set_from_array_acc[T: Eq] (arr: Array[T]) (i: Int) (acc: Set[T]): Set[T] =\0A    if i >= arr.len() { acc } else { set_from_array_acc(arr, i + 1, Set.insert(acc, arr[i])) }\0A/// A set of the array's elements, discarding duplicates.\0Apub let Set.from_array[T: Eq] (arr: Array[T]): Set[T] = set_from_array_acc(arr, 0, Set.new(()))\0A\0A// --- JSON ---\0A\0A/// Mirrored from `crates/plumc/src/lib.rs`'s STDLIB_JSON_SRC, minus its\0A/// own `chars_of` (the runtime already provides that one, since it is the\0A/// single thing this prelude cannot write in Plum). Everything else is\0A/// character-for-character the real compiler's, so the two agree on\0A/// number formatting, escape handling and parse errors by construction\0A/// rather than by inspection.\0Apub struct JsonEntry { pub key: String, pub value: JsonValue }\0A/// A parsed JSON document.\0A///\0A/// Numbers are binary64 `Float`s and may lose precision when parsed.\0A/// `use Json;` brings in decoders that turn one of these into your own\0A/// types; `Json.int` rejects values outside the safe integer range.\0Apub enum JsonValue {\0A    JsonNull,\0A    JsonBool(Bool),\0A    JsonNumber(Float),\0A    JsonString(String),\0A    JsonArray(Array[JsonValue]),\0A    JsonObject(Array[JsonEntry]),\0A}\0A/// What a JSON parse step consumed: a value, and where to carry on\0A/// from. Public because the parser's own pieces return it; a caller\0A/// wants `json_parse`.\0Apub struct ParseResult[T] { pub value: T, pub next_pos: Int }\0A\0Alet skip_ws (chars: Array[String]) (pos: Int): Int =\0A    if pos >= chars.len() { pos }\0A    else {\0A        let c = chars[pos];\0A        if c == \22 \22 || c == \22\5Ct\22 || c == \22\5Cn\22 || c == \22\5Cr\22 { skip_ws(chars, pos + 1) } else { pos }\0A    }\0A\0Alet digit_value (c: String): Result[Float, String] =\0A    if c == \220\22 { Ok(0.0) }\0A    else if c == \221\22 { Ok(1.0) }\0A    else if c == \222\22 { Ok(2.0) }\0A    else if c == \223\22 { Ok(3.0) }\0A    else if c == \224\22 { Ok(4.0) }\0A    else if c == \225\22 { Ok(5.0) }\0A    else if c == \226\22 { Ok(6.0) }\0A    else if c == \227\22 { Ok(7.0) }\0A    else if c == \228\22 { Ok(8.0) }\0A    else if c == \229\22 { Ok(9.0) }\0A    else { Err(\22not a digit\22) }\0A\0Alet pow10 (n: Int): Float = if n <= 0 { 1.0 } else { pow10(n - 1) * 1